\section*{Finite cylinder approximation of ruled sets}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that compact ruled segment families under the stated hyperbolic or square-zero differential conditions admit finite-cylinder approximations within any positive error of their integral projection cost.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), each coarse square freezes the field to an affine map, while a finer square lattice captures nonlinear target points with only a thin collar. Cost is summed with multiplicity; logarithmic product cells provide an alternative when the parameter boundary has zero area.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: compact ruled segment families under the stated hyperbolic or square-zero differential conditions admit finite-cylinder approximations within any positive error of their integral projection cost.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CylinderCovering.lean}, \texttt{RuledCovering.lean}, \texttt{TriangularCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CylinderCovering.fourPaperMain}, \texttt{OAI.RuledApproximation.fullMain}, \texttt{OAI.TriangularCovering.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

